**Corollary 7.1 (actual whole-row obstruction and conditional recovery).**
Under Assumption 1.4, a positive all-depth answer for any selected $|B|\ge2$
rules out a uniform finite-new-read recovery service for the whole row on
the entire $D$, including lawful adaptive serial forward updates.
Sourcewise finite original traversal at the actual certified $N$ still
terminates. In particular the entire eight-hole input has this obstruction
at $N=2$.

If the exact entire attained row image $A=R_Q(D)$ is finite, every distinct
pair is exhaustively handled by the recognized class or an owning accepted
alternative, and every such pair has a finite exclusion, then whole-row
recovery has an effective finite original-prefix bound. With $H$ the maximum
pair exclusion depth, the existing current-register decoder gives

$$
q\le2(2^{H+1}-1),\qquad a=0,\qquad
\max |p|\le H+\lceil N/2\rceil.
\tag{NIS.29}
$$

Proof. The first assertion is the ARH original-prefix/static/serial existence
correspondence (ARH.2.3): every finite depth admits distinct full rows at one
common prefix. Its sourcewise alternative uses the paid current-register
decoder to traverse the original finite tree and then compute its whole row.
It yields no uniform call cap.

For the positive recovery assertion, NPL Theorem 5.3 supplies the exact finite
whole-row image under its full-input hypotheses. Depth $H$ separates every
distinct pair. Decode all $2^{H+1}-1$ original replies through $H$ using
DelayedOriginalReadout.original_decoder_service, with at most two actual
current reads per original reply and the displayed address bound. A local
NSF query with that acquired prefix and all input constraints yields a finite
candidate; its whole row is forced. The candidate is not a free physical
source selection. Total submitted literal length is at most the call bound
times the word bound. Preparation, local computation and memory retain
their separate charges. $\square$

